llm_extracted_info.methodology:
The core methodology is Token-Weighted Learning (TWL), which modifies the standard perplexity objective function by assigning dynamic weights (0 < wi 
<= 1) to each token sequence, approximating its importance. Token importance is defined as "usefulness," the expected utility of the token in related future tasks. A meta-learning framework is used to train "Train-Attention" (TA), a supportive model (meta-learner, 



) that predicts these token weights. TA inherits the architecture of a causal LM but its decoder layer is modified to output a single-dimensional float [0,1] for each token position. The meta-learning process optimizes TA (



) to minimize the task loss of the base model (u) after u has been updated using TA's predicted weights. The base model u is reset to its initial state after each 



 update. TA (



) can be based on small LLMs (e.g., TinyLlama-1.1B) or bidirectional transformers like BERT (101M parameters) for resource efficiency, being compatible with various base models that share the same tokenizer.

